Title: FIELD STATION
Credit: Written by
Author: The Method Storyteller contributors
Draft date: Sample script
Contact: An invented script for the Method Production Suite's pictures and tests.

EXT. SALT FLATS - DAWN

Flat white ground to every horizon. A single prefab hut, a weather mast and a dented pickup truck. The wind turns the mast's cups and nothing else moves.

Behind the hut a generator coughs, catches and settles into a hum.

INT. FIELD STATION - MAIN ROOM - CONTINUOUS

One room doing the work of five: bunks, a galley, a radio desk, and a long bench of instruments under dust covers.

NORA VELASCO (40s), sun-creased and precise, peels the cover off a seismograph. The drum is still. She taps the glass.

NORA
Come on. You were talking all night.

She checks the paper roll. Blank. She checks the cable at the back of the bench. It's been cut, neatly.

NORA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Okay.

The bunk curtain rattles open. EMMETT HALE (20s), a graduate student who has never once woken up on time, squints at her.

EMMETT
Is it the drum again? I can rewind it.

NORA
It's not the drum.

She holds up the cut end of the cable. Emmett is suddenly awake.

EMMETT
That was fine at midnight. I logged it.

NORA
Then show me the log.

INT. FIELD STATION - RADIO DESK - MOMENTS LATER

Emmett pages through a battered logbook. Nora leans over his shoulder.

EMMETT
Twenty-three fifty, cable checked, readings normal. Oh one hundred, readings normal. Oh two hundred...

He stops. The line after oh two hundred is in different handwriting.

NORA
That's not yours.

EMMETT
That's not anybody's. There's two of us.

Nora picks up the radio handset and keys it.

NORA
(into radio)
Base, this is Field Station. Did you send anyone out overnight? Over.

Static. Then a calm, tired voice.

DISPATCHER (V.O.)
Negative, Field Station. Nobody's within sixty miles of you. Everything all right? Over.

Nora looks at the logbook. Looks at Emmett.

NORA
(into radio)
Everything's fine. We'll call at noon. Out.

EMMETT
Everything's fine?

NORA
Everything's fine until we know what isn't.

EXT. SALT FLATS - DAY

The sun is up and merciless. Nora walks the cable line from the hut, coiling slack as she goes. Emmett follows with a clipboard.

Fifty yards out, she stops. In the crust: footprints. Bare feet, small, walking toward the hut and never back.

EMMETT
Those are a kid's.

NORA
Those are somebody's.

She crouches. The prints are sharp-edged, fresh. She follows them with her eyes back toward the horizon, where heat shimmer turns the ground to water.

EMMETT
We should call it in.

NORA
And say what?

EMMETT
(beat)
That the log's wrong and the ground's wrong.

Nora stands, dusts her knees.

NORA
Get the camera. We photograph everything, then we call.

INT. FIELD STATION - MAIN ROOM - NIGHT

The generator hums. A single lamp. Emmett has fallen asleep at the radio desk, head on his arms.

Nora sits at the bench with the seismograph, the cable spliced and taped. The drum turns slowly. The needle draws a flat line.

Then it twitches. Once. Twice. A small, regular rhythm, like footsteps.

Nora doesn't move. She watches the needle walk across the paper, getting stronger.

The rhythm stops, right outside the door.

A long silence. The lamp buzzes.

Nora reaches over and gently shakes Emmett awake. She puts a finger to her lips and points at the door.

Somebody KNOCKS. Three times, politely.

CUT TO BLACK.

THE END
